    {
      "title": "India's current account deficit narrows to $5.2 billion in August: RBI data",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/india-s-current-account-deficit-narrows-to-5-2-billion-in-august-rbi-data-126101501460_1.html",
      "description": "India's current account deficit narrowed to $5.2 billion in August from $6.1 billion a year ago, while strong banking capital inflows helped the BoP post a $45.4 billion surplus",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-15T15:09:33"
    },
    {
      "title": "Current account deficit narrows to $5.2 billion in August: RBI data",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/current-account-deficit-narrows-to-5-2-billion-in-august-rbi-data-126101501194_1.html",
      "description": "India's current account deficit narrowed to USD 5.2 billion in August from USD 6.1 billion a year ago, aided by a lower merchandise trade deficit and higher surpluses in services trade, according to preliminary data released by the RBI on Thursday.\nThe net deficit in the merchandise trade account declined to USD 26.6 billion in August from USD 27.6 billion in the same month last year.\nMerchandise exports rose to USD 44.7 billion in August from USD 35.1 billion a year ago, while imports increased to USD 71.3 billion from USD 62.7 billion, the RBI data showed.\nThe surplus in services trade increased to USD 16.5 billion in August from USD 15.6 billion a year ago. Services exports rose to USD 35.5 billion from USD 31.2 billion, while imports increased to USD 18.9 billion from USD 15.6 billion.\nNet transfers, including remittances, increased to USD 12.8 billion in August from USD 12.2 billion a year ago, while the net deficit in the income account widened to USD 8 billion from USD 6.3 .",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-15T13:24:59"
    },
    {
      "title": "India's trade deficit widens to $30.21 bn in Sep from $26.86 bn in August",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/india-trade-deficit-september-exports-imports-commerce-ministry-126101500744_1.html",
      "description": "Merchandise exports jumped 23 per cent year-on-year to a four-month high of $44.40 billion, while imports rose nearly 11 per cent to $74.61 billion",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-15T09:39:52"
    },
    {
      "title": "India's exports rise 23% to $44.4 bn in September, trade deficit narrows",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/india-s-exports-rise-23-to-44-4-bn-in-september-trade-deficit-narrows-126101500664_1.html",
      "description": "India's exports increased by 23 per cent to USD 44.4 billion in September, and the trade deficit narrowed to USD 30.21 billion, government data released on Thursday said.\nThe country's imports rose by 8 per cent to USD 74.61 billion in September.\nIndia's trade deficit in September 2025 stood at USD 33 billion.\nDuring April-September this fiscal, exports jumped 18.51 per cent to USD 259.89 billion, and imports climbed 16.31 per cent to USD 437.53 billion.\nExports are maintaining their growth trend, Commerce Secretary Rajesh Agarwal said in a press briefing on trade data.",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-15T09:17:34"
    },
    {
      "title": "World Bank Group mobilises $36 bn in India in 4 yrs, $9.6 bn for MSMEs",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/news/world-bank-group-mobilises-36-bn-in-india-in-4-yrs-9-6-bn-for-msmes-126101500458_1.html",
      "description": "The World Bank Group has helped mobilise USD 36 billion in private capital for development in India over the past four years, including USD 9.6 billion for Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs), as it steps up efforts to spur private sector-led growth and job creation.\nPrivate capital mobilisation by the Group rose nearly five-fold to USD 14.5 billion in fiscal 2026 from about USD 3 billion in fiscal 2022, Manuel Reyes-Retana, IFC's division director for South Asia, said.\nThe increase reflects growing investor interest in development-linked investments, with guarantees from the International Finance Corporation (IFC) and the Multilateral Investment Guarantee Agency (MIGA) helping reduce investment risks and attract private funding, he said.\nInstitutional investors are increasingly drawn to such instruments because they are familiar with the World Bank Group's underwriting standards and governance practices, Reyes-Retana said.\n\"We are seeing a positive response from private ..",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-15T08:00:00"
    }
  ]
}